{"name":"Prompt","hex":"#5e7fb5","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"prompt","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Quick; acting without delay.","On time; punctual."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A reminder or cue.\n A word, phrase or line supplied by a prompter to an actor who has forgotten the script.\n A suggestion for inspiration given to an author.\n A sequence of characters that is displayed to indicate that a computer is ready to receive input.","A word, phrase or line supplied by a prompter to an actor who has forgotten the script."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/prompt"}],"entries":[{"title":"Command-line interface","blocks":[{"text":"A command-line interface (CLI), also known as a command-line shell, is a means of interacting with software via commands – each formatted as a line of text. The concept of interacting with a computer via text evolved over two decades, transitioning from physical hardware to sophisticated software. Before the CLI, computers were programmed using physical switches or punched cards. The shift toward a \"command\" style interaction began with the use of teleprinters/teletypes. Early systems like the Whirlwind I (1951) at MIT began utilizing typewriter-like inputs for direct control, moving away from batch processing where you'd hand a stack of cards to an operator and wait hours for a result. Devices like the Teletype Model 33, introduced in 1963, allowed operators to type a command and receive a printed response from the computer."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/29/Linux_command-line._Bash._GNOME_Terminal._screenshot.png/330px-Linux_command-line._Bash._GNOME_Terminal._screenshot.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Command-line_interface","wikidata":"Q189053"},{"title":"Command Prompt (Windows)","blocks":[{"text":"Command Prompt, or Windows Command Processor, is a shell program on later versions of Windows, OS/2, eComStation, ArcaOS, and ReactOS. In some versions of Windows it is referred to as the Command Processor Shell. Implementation differs between operating systems, but with significant consistency of behavior and available commands."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Command_Prompt_(Windows)","wikidata":"Q310897"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d5/Windows_PowerShell_1.0_PD.png/500px-Windows_PowerShell_1.0_PD.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d5/Windows_PowerShell_1.0_PD.png/500px-Windows_PowerShell_1.0_PD.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Screenshot of Windows PowerShell 1.0, running on Windows Vista ","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AWindows_PowerShell_1.0_PD.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6b/Gem_11_Desktop.png/500px-Gem_11_Desktop.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6b/Gem_11_Desktop.png/500px-Gem_11_Desktop.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A graphical user interface with icons and windows (GEM 1.1 Desktop)","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AGem_11_Desktop.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ad/GNUOctave430.png/500px-GNUOctave430.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ad/GNUOctave430.png/1280px-GNUOctave430.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ad/GNUOctave430.png/500px-GNUOctave430.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"GNU Octave's GUI with co